1. INTRODUCTION
1.1 Purpose of the SDD
This Software Design Document (SDD) describes the architectural and detailed design of the AI-Based Study Assistant Chatbot, an intelligent web application providing real-time academic assistance to students through a conversational interface. The document translates the requirements defined in the Software Requirements Specification (SRS) into a concrete, implementable software design.
This SDD serves the following audiences:
Software Engineers — as a blueprint for implementation of frontend, backend, database, and integration layers.
Quality Assurance Engineers — as a reference for designing test cases mapped to architectural components.
DevOps Engineers — as a guide for infrastructure provisioning, deployment, and monitoring.
Project Stakeholders & Supervisors — as evidence that the requirements have been thoughtfully transformed into a viable technical solution.
Future Maintainers — as institutional knowledge for evolution and extension of the system.
The document adheres to IEEE Std 1016-2009 (Standard for Information Technology — Systems Design — Software Design Descriptions), while pragmatically extending it with modern web-architecture concerns such as containerization, CI/CD, and AI service integration.
1.2 Scope of the System
The system being designed is a multi-tenant, single-page web application that enables authenticated students to converse with a Large Language Model (LLM) via a moderated, context-aware chat interface. The scope includes:
In-Scope:
Student account lifecycle (registration → email verification → login → profile management → password reset).
Real-time messaging UI with token-by-token streaming responses.
Persistent, searchable chat history organized into conversations.
Server-side integration with the OpenAI Chat Completions API (default provider) with provider-abstraction for future swaps.
Prompt sanitization, content moderation, and context-window management.
An administrative subsystem for user management, dashboard metrics, and flagged-content review.
Responsive UI compatible with viewports 320px–1920px+.
Out-of-Scope (deferred to future versions):
Multimedia generation/ingestion (images, audio, video).
Native mobile applications (the responsive web app serves mobile needs).
LMS integration (Moodle, Canvas, etc.).
Offline-first capabilities.
Real-time human tutoring or video conferencing.
