2. SYSTEM OVERVIEW
2.1 System Context & Background
In contemporary higher education, the asynchronous nature of self-study creates a “knowledge gap window” — students encounter conceptual obstacles outside of formal class hours and lack immediate scaffolding to overcome them. Existing solutions (e.g., search engines, static FAQs, peer forums) suffer from low precision, latency, or social friction. The emergence of capable LLMs makes it feasible to deliver a personalized, conversational tutor at scale.
The AI-Based Study Assistant Chatbot positions itself as a thin, opinionated layer on top of a general-purpose LLM, with three distinguishing characteristics:
Academic Framing — Through a curated system prompt (FR-19), the assistant is constrained to a pedagogical persona, reducing the likelihood of off-topic or inappropriate responses.
Conversation Continuity — Persistent, per-user history (FR-13–FR-17) enables longitudinal learning rather than disconnected one-shot queries.
Safety & Privacy by Design — PII stripping (FR-18) and content moderation (FR-12) protect both the student and the institution.
