4. ARCHITECTURAL DESIGN
4.1 Chosen Architectural Style
After evaluation of alternatives, the system adopts a Layered (N-Tier) Architecture with a Modular Monolith backend, fronted by a Single-Page Application (SPA).
Alternatives considered:
Style
Suitability
Verdict
Microservices
Over-engineered for a 100-user, 3-table system; increases ops complexity
❌ Rejected
Serverless (FaaS)
Cold-start latency conflicts with NFR-01 (≤3s); LLM streaming awkward over Lambda
❌ Rejected
Modular Monolith (chosen)
Single deployable unit; clear module boundaries; horizontally scalable behind a load balancer; ideal for small team
✅ Adopted
Event-Driven / CQRS
Unnecessary complexity — no high-throughput event firehose
❌ Rejected

The monolith is internally modularized (Auth, Chat, History, AI-Integration, Admin), with each module exposing a service interface. Should the system ever need to scale beyond a monolith, these modules can be extracted into independent services with minimal refactoring.
